STRING(3)                      P32W Unix                       STRING(3)

NAME
       string, strlen, strcpy, strncpy, strcat, strncat - copy strings

SYNOPSIS
       #include <string.h>

       size_t strlen(const char *s);
       char *strcpy(char *d, const char *s);
       char *strncpy(char *d, const char *s, size_t n);
       char *strcat(char *d, const char *s);
       char *strncat(char *d, const char *s, size_t n);

DESCRIPTION
       A string is an array of characters ended by a NUL ('\0').
       string.h declares the functions on strings and on blocks of
       memory:

       string(3)    strlen, strcpy, strncpy, strcat, strncat
       strcmp(3)    strcmp, strncmp
       strchr(3)    strchr, strrchr, strstr, strpbrk
       strspn(3)    strspn, strcspn, strtok
       strdup(3)    strdup
       perror(3)    strerror
       memory(3)    memcpy, memmove, memset, memcmp, memchr

       strlen returns the number of characters in s, not counting
       the NUL.

       strcpy copies s, NUL included, to d. strcat copies s to the
       end of the string d. Both return d. d must have room; the
       strings must not overlap.

       strncpy copies at most n characters of s to d. If s is
       shorter than n, the rest of d's n bytes are filled with NULs;
       if s has n characters or more, d is NOT ended with a NUL.

       strncat appends at most n characters of s to the string d,
       and then always a NUL (so d needs strlen(d) + n + 1 bytes).

RETURN VALUE
       strlen returns the length; the others return d.

EXAMPLES
       char path[PATH_MAX];        /* from limits.h: 256 */
       strcpy(path, dir);
       strcat(path, "/");
       strncat(path, name, sizeof path - strlen(path) - 1);

SEE ALSO
       strcmp(3), strchr(3), strspn(3), strdup(3), memory(3)

BUGS
       Nothing checks the size of d. There is no strlcpy, strlcat,
       index or rindex, no strcoll or strxfrm, and no strcasecmp.
